  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_down]` is on an explicit function, and its fenced commentary gives a substantive reason. The source supports each part of that reason:\n\n- **Test-only code:** the function sits in `mod tests`, which is marked `#[cfg(test)]`, so it is compiled only for unit tests.\n- **Required callback:** it is a `TermLike` trait method on the test adapter `RecordingTerm`, so the adapter has to define it. Its body is `Ok(())`, so it has no application behavior and no input path.\n- **Single-line displays:** `Progress::with_target` builds only single-line templates, such as the `{spinner} {msg} [...]` forms, and no multi-line layout. This agrees with the claim that the displays never move the cursor down.\n- **Real terminal drawing:** `tests/authoring_batch.rs` has PTY helpers (`run_in_pty`, `run_in_pty_with_args`), and several tests drive the real terminal output through them.\n\nThe commentary is fenced, so it counts as tracked justification. It is not an ordinary comment, and it is not justified by difficulty alone. The citation is not redundant, because the function has no `impl` or `test` reference to the same name. It names no project requirement, so the usual role rules do not apply. I found no gap.",
